1. THE CONDITION, AND WHO IT NEVER REACHES

This license gives You every right the MIT License gives You,
granted in Section 2, on one condition beyond MIT's: if products
and services that contain the Software, derive from it, or provide
its functionality to others by running it bring You, together with
every entity under common control with You, more than
US $1,000,000 in a calendar year, You pay Licensor 3% of the
amount above US $1,000,000. Almost nobody owes it. Where the
Software is one component of something larger, only the share
fairly attributable to it counts.

Under that line You owe nothing and condition (b) of Section 2 is
satisfied for the year with no act on Your part: nothing to pay,
report, register, or notify. Keep the copyright notice in copies,
as condition (a) requires, and You are finished with this license.
The line is tested year by year, so look again once a year. If You
are not sure whether something You sell is covered, Section 5
makes the answer free and binding within 30 days.

Crossing the line never makes the first US $1,000,000 payable,
never reaches back into an earlier year, and never ends this
license. Sections 4 through 6 say how Covered Revenue is counted
and the fee is paid. This section describes the condition; Section
2 states it and Sections 4 through 6 measure it. Where this
section and those differ, those govern, and nothing in this
section creates an obligation they do not.


